        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "41207e2d-8813-5931-a6f1-0b9afa3da9fe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Clement Greenberg, Agatha Christie, Joseph Drapell, Jack Bush\n- Organizations: Cranbrook Academy, Ben's Bakery\n- Roles: visual art critic, essayist, crime novelist, short story writer, playwright, painter, artist\n- Topics: American Modern art, Life (sculpture)\n\nFacts:\n- Agatha Christie was born on 15 September 1890 as Dame Agatha Mary Clarissa Christie, née Miller, and became an English crime novelist, short story writer, and playwright.\n- Clement Greenberg was born on 16 January 1909, sometimes wrote under the pseudonym K. Hardesh, and became an American essayist and influential visual art critic associated with mid-20th-century American Modern art.\n- In 1968, Ben's Bakery commissioned Life, a sculpture produced by painter Joseph Drapell.\n- At Cranbrook Academy, Joseph Drapell met visiting Canadian artist Jack Bush and American art critic Clement Greenberg, and both influenced Joseph Drapell's work.\n- Agatha Christie died on 12 January 1976.\n- Clement Greenberg died on 7 May 1994.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
